/-!
# Stellmacher Section Nine

This module combines the numbered commuting-critical-pair results with the
proved local inputs to (9.10). The retained two-extraction configuration gives
the terminal order-32 module, wreath-product quotient and order-eight
preterminal intersection. The literal initial-center commutator supplies a
centralizing neighbor and its normalizer index. The distance-two neighborhood
join supplies the core containment, invariance and long-path abelianness used
by the remaining critical-distance contradictions.

The long-distance argument bounds the critical length by five. The normal
predecessor intersection, two-step classification, and second-core action
support the distance-five contradiction. The first module lies in
its own distance-two join. A nontrivial two-subgroup normalized by a vertex
stabilizer has solvable ambient normalizer by the original Hypothesis Two;
this ambient transfer is also used in Section Ten.
The finite wreath selector supplies the original good neighbor; its full
neighborhood centralizes the preterminal module and lies in the terminal
stabilizer. Normal-subgroup displacement gives the exact residual escape
needed by the length-five contradiction. Normal neighborhood generation and
the quotient-kernel argument supply its final subgroup comparisons.
The two cases of the distance-five contradiction use residual displacement
and the fixed space of a nontrivial normal subgroup of the actual Sylow
action. Their assembly proves the principal (9.10) bound by three, both for
the ambient context and through the original canonical wrapper.
Source: Stellmacher, Journal of Algebra 190 (1997), Section Nine, printed
pp.46–59, with the orbit-dependent neighborhood definition on p.50.
-/
